 "theory-subtypes": {
  "id": "PVTI_lAHOAAfgFs4BsfrazhLaJeQ",
  "title": "A type with a condition is a subtype, and what…",
  "updated": "2026-11-24T15:38:06Z",
  "key": "theory-subtypes",
  "agent": "",
  "try": "",
  "repeat": "",
  "now_by_todo": "2026-11-24T13:40:48Z",
  "assignees": [],
  "column": "Done",
  "column_since": "2026-11-24T15:18:11Z",
  "content": {
   "__typename": "Issue",
   "id": "I_kwDOV1nZQs8AAAABiCXNmg",
   "title": "A type with a condition is a subtype, and what…",
   "body": "Original title: A type with a condition is a subtype, and what the compiler cannot prove it checks at run time\n\nMilestone E4 (plan 3.2, 3.9): where, type X < T, conversions, obligations: prove or check. char is text where #it == 1. plan: notes/coc-plan.md (branch coc-plan until it lands); the files and what each gives the others: notes/theory.md.\n\nAdded by wap-coc-plan with `todo add`.\n\nBoard card: https://github.com/users/pannous/projects/10?pane=issue&itemId=316286436\n\nbranch: theory-subtypes (2026-11-24)\n\nPLAN (wap-simplifier, for wap-supervisor). Base: theory-traits-conditions (E3, e22b6438); branch from its tip, merge main once it lands. Core and kernel cost: 0; everything in wap/extensions/theory/elaborate/ and prelude/. New files convert.wap and obligations.wap land with \"spec: wiki/subtype.md\".\nOne mechanism, the same as traits: a proof is a field. A subset value and a trait instance are both structures, each with a proof field. Every proof field the elaborator has to fill is one kind of obligation, discharged by obligations.wap. So Finite(T)'s law `shrinks` and char's `#it == 1` go through the same code.\n1. prelude/data.wap: `class Subset(T: Sort(u), p: T -> Prop) { value: T; proof: p(value) }`. The enum in checked.wap moves here, and checked.wap uses it.\n2. read.wap: `T where c`, where T elaborates to a type, is Subset(T, it => c). `where` on a list stays the filter: told apart by the first argument, like any overload (E3 fitting). declare.wap: `type N(params) = T` is a definition whose value is a type, so vector and index of plan 3.3 come out of it.\n3. `type X < T`: a one-field class X { value: T }, a new type with no condition, plus its conversion up.\n4. convert.wap: a conversion is an instance of the prelude trait `Converts(A, B) { convert: A -> B }`, found by E3's instance search. There is no list of conversions in the elaborator. The prelude gives:\n   - Subset(T, p) to T: one instance that covers every subset;\n   - nat to int: the number tower.\n   `type X < T` gives X's instance. Cases and unions come in E7 the same way.\n   Fitting (E3) tries unify first, then a conversion path. The path must be the only shortest one, else the refusal `conversion` names both. Lifting through list, T? and a map's values uses a Mappable trait instance (map). That is a trait too, not a container list.\n5. obligations.wap: where a Subset(T, p) is wanted and a T is given, the term is Subset.intro(v, ?proof). ?proof is the obligation p(v), discharged in plan 3.9 order:\n   (1) compute: the Decidable of p(v), found by E3's `decided`, computes to yes. The proof is the prelude theorem decide_sound(p, d, h: decide(p, d) == yes): p. If it computes to no: refusal `condition`.\n   (2) facts in scope: E3's branch facts (variables typed by the condition), plus `.proof` of every Subset-typed variable in scope, which covers a parameter's own condition. Unify against them.\n   (3) provers: nothing yet (P2).\n   (4) a run-time check, only for `x as S`: prelude `checked(T, p, d, x): Fallible(Subset(T, p))`. An implicit going-down with no proof is the refusal `unproved condition` until E5's effects carry Fail. Then the same step-4 term gets Fail, and `effects of` shows it.\n   (5) refusal `unproved condition`, naming the condition and the fixes.\n   `x is S` is decide(p(x)).\n   Trait laws (Finite's `shrinks`, Comparable's laws) use steps 1-3 only. A law is never a run-time check. If none works: `unproved law`, the same as today.\n6. Tests: test/wap/test_theory_subtypes.wap holds the lines of plan 3.2 and 3.3 with their stated outcomes:\n   - c: char = \"a\" fine; \"ab\" refused by `condition`;\n   - shout(c) converts;\n   - `if #t == 1 { e: char = t }` fine by the fact;\n   - initial(name: text where #it > 0);\n   - dot of two vector(number, 3) is 32; dot with lengths 3 and 2 refused;\n   - append's vector(T, m + n).\n   test_theory_subtypes_wanted.wap holds the lines that need E5: read_line, `effects of` a check = Fail.\nOrder: Subset + where + type definitions; then conversions up (convert.wap); then obligations steps 1-2; then `as`/`is`; then `< T`. Each is its own commit. 2 asks for the E3 fixes (Decidable and trait_of refusing ambiguity) first, since conversion search has the same need.\nOpen, for the supervisor: should the Fallible step-4 term land now, or wait for E5? My recommendation is now, explicit `as` only.\n\nPlan approved (wap-supervisor). Decision: Fallible(Subset) for explicit 'x as S' lands now; implicit downcast without proof refused until E5. One shared ambiguity refusal with E3.\n\nland: theory-subtypes 4ec67e89 — x is S decides the condition (a bool); a definition naming it is a type. Left: x as S waits for E5 Fallible; type X < T waits for type declarations.\n\nsupervisor: advisor on 4ec67e89: sound (every obligation discharged by a kernel-checked term). Fixes sent: shared subset_parts (obligations.wap:47 looked through function types), refuse when two facts fit, CONDITION counts as no-fit for overloads (decided: char/text overload with \"ab\" picks text; UNPROVED stays loud), own rule for non-subset is, notes/elaborate.md:190.\n\nLeft (plan 3.2/3.3, notes/coc-plan.md): char lines — `type char = text where #it == 1`, `c: char = \"a\"`, `d: char = \"ab\"` refused, `shout(c)` free, `if #line == 1 { e: char = line }`, `f = line as char` (waits for E5's check), `initial(name: text where #it > 0): char`; vector lines — `type vector(T, n: nat) = list<T> where #it == n`, `index(xs)`, `matrix(m, n)`, `append`'s vector(T, m + n), `multiply`, `at(xs, i: index(xs))`; dot — `dot([1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6])` is 32 and `dot([1, 2, 3], [4, 5])` refused naming two lengths. Also `type X < T` (needs type declarations) and `nat where it == 1` (operators in a condition).\n\nsupervisor: c79b00ec sound, all fixes in (computed_subset shared; two facts refusal; CONDITION no-fit; not a subtype; notes). Sent to integrator.\n\nuser OK 2026-11-24 (sugar, 0 core, spec: wiki/subtype.md) via wap-interviewer: the rule type X(…) = T → X(…) := T lands.\n\nFixed in [7df5fa16](https://github.com/pannous/wap/commit/7df5fa1658f4cfe108d98091ed53999e6d406a36): merge theory-subtypes: `x is S` decides S's condition at x, a definition whose condition names it is a type (E4; wap-supervisor sound)\n\nland: theory-subtypes-b 76c50045 (list literal [a, b] → list cases; with wap-integrator). Wiki Footguns 'Bytes vs graphemes' rewritten (wiki 210ed67). Left: (1) sugar rule `type X(…) = T` → `X(…) := T` (user-approved, spec: wiki/subtype.md), not started; (2) int(c)/c as int refusal: a plain overload in prelude.wap catches int(\"7\") (a one-char literal is a char), so it is out; options are with wap-interviewer; (3) plan 3.2/3.3 lines char/vector/dot wait for theory-operators-b + (1). The theory-subtypes worktree can go once E4 (c79b00ec) is on main.\n\nReleased 2026-11-24 by wap-simplifier: done: E4 c79b00ec, list literal on theory-subtypes-b 76c50045 (with integrator), wiki Footguns; left: type X = T sugar, int(c) refusal (interviewer), plan lines char/vector/dot; branch: theory-subtypes-b\n\nSupervisor: E4-b 76c50045 is sound (list literal). Follow-up: case_given's declaration(…)! should refuse with a fix-it when the program's own list lacks item/empty, not trap. The char tasks (type sugar, int(c) refusal, Footguns #) are not in this tip.",
   "state": "CLOSED",
   "assignees": {
    "nodes": []
   },
