  "content": {
   "__typename": "Issue",
   "id": "I_kwDOV1nZQs8AAAABiMN4zw",
   "title": "A program using c fails with \"cannot read…",
   "body": "Original title: A program using c fails with \"cannot read types/host.wap\" unless compiled from inside the compiler's folder\n\nRepro: printf 'use c\\nprint 1\\n' > p.wap; from /Users/me/dev/angles/wap, warp --no-window <wap1 of origin/main 578bbee7>/wap1.wasm p.wap out.wasm → cannot read types/host.wap. It works when run from inside the compiler's folder; `use foreign` works everywhere. Path: c.warp's foreign-rule output names words of lib/foreign.wap, which standard_brought (core/pipeline.wap) brings in by name; that module's `use \"../types/host.wap\"` comes out relative to the compiler folder. Blocks wap-fixer's native-c-records test. Found by wap-binding; not class-inheritance (both of its changed core paths reverted, still red).\n\nAdded by wap-binding with `todo add`.\n\nBoard card: https://github.com/users/pannous/projects/10?pane=issue&itemId=316928053\n\nbranch: use-c-relative-path (2026-11-25)\n\nland: use-c-relative-path e25bc1c2. Cause: test/wap/compile.sh wrote the warm state with a relative compiler path (folder '.'), so extensions/warm.wap marked no path; fixed by the full path. test_c 22/22 on the Mac.\n\nFixed in [45e4f156](https://github.com/pannous/wap/commit/45e4f15663d41a28100ee7c1bd04b91a38a9d7b3): merge use-c-relative-path: a test compiler's warm state knows the wap folder by its full path; run.sh --known reads the reds by file\n\ndup?: use-fixit-path (by score, 2026-11-27)",
