   "id": "I_kwDOV1nZQs8AAAABhgHfgg",
   "title": "native host: macOS only, Windows and x86-64 Linux…",
   "body": "Original title: The native host runs only on macOS: Windows, x86-64 Linux C calls and Linux window surfaces are missing.\n\nMoved from warp cards windows-native, x86-linux, gpu-metal-layer. (1) Windows: the libc calls in lib/native/foreign.c (dlopen/dlsym, popen, posix_spawn) need LoadLibrary/GetProcAddress, _popen, CreateProcess in one place; the release matrix gets windows x86_64; test on the user's Windows box. (2) x86-64 Linux: lib/host/native_host/gpu.warp passes WGPU*CallbackInfo (40 bytes) by pointer, AAPCS64 style; SysV wants it on the stack: a struct parameter kind in native_call. (3) Linux window: the GPU window shows only in a Metal layer; give it an X11/Wayland surface (SDL3 window properties); needs Linux CI. Old card: todo show <key> in /Users/me/dev/angles/warp (warp board https://github.com/users/pannous/projects/1), archived in the 11-22 board sweep.\n\nAdded by warp-board-sweep with `todo add`.\n\nBoard card: https://github.com/users/pannous/projects/10?pane=issue&itemId=314145335\n\npart (2) x86-64 struct by value: wap/types/c.wap c_struct's TODO names this card; the unused lib/native/foreign.c native_call_struct (warp's, a 128-byte aligned carrier the C compiler places right on SysV and AAPCS64) left on branch struct-by-value-everywhere, recoverable from git if wanted\n\nLinux (cloud run 2026-11-25, main 76d89f5, clang 18, wabt 1.0.42): build.sh's -std=c23 hides POSIX on glibc, so wasm-rt-impl.c misses sigaction/stack_t/sigaltstack/SS_DISABLE/SIGSTKSZ (needs -std=gnu23 or _POSIX_C_SOURCE; extension change, user OK). wabt 1.0.42's wasm2c also writes 'u32 u32 var_i0' (fixed at ce4ebe80, which the Mac uses). Also -rpath @executable_path is macOS-only ($ORIGIN on Linux). The cloud full-suite routine builds wabt ce4ebe80 and falls back to the installed wap until this builds.\n\nbranch: native-other-platforms (2026-11-25)\n\nLinux build flags on branch native-other-platforms bf74edfc (-std=gnu23; rpath named once in scripts/lib/wap-beside.sh wap_linked, $ORIGIN on Linux). Mac: test_hello, test_native 59/59 green. Waiting for the user's OK via wap-interviewer (extension change), then to wap-integrator. Parts (1) Windows, (2) x86-64 struct by value, (3) Linux window stay open on this card.\n\nuser OK 2026-11-25 (extension) via wap-interviewer: land bf74edfc (build.sh -std=gnu23; rpath named once in scripts/lib/wap-beside.sh)\n\nLinux build fix landed 625564b8 (bf74edfc: -std=gnu23, rpath $ORIGIN via wap_linked); Windows, x86-64 struct and Linux window parts remain\n\nPart 2 (x86-64 struct by value) waits on use-c-relative-path: every test_c check is red on both systems ('cannot read types/host.wap'). Design: a struct<n> kind from the foreign rule through extensions/c/functions.wap into native_call and glue.awk, about 40-60 lines, needs a user OK. Checked by test_c in the Linux run.\n\nuser OK 2026-11-25 (extension) via wap-interviewer: C structs by value on x86-64 (struct sizes as their own kind in the C function table; ~+40-60 extensions/c, lib/native, lib; 0 core); build now\n\npart 2 built: branch native-other-platforms e1de6cbe, waits for wiki/c.md bytes<N> line (interviewer) so compiler-growth passes with spec:\n\nuser 2026-11-25 via wap-interviewer: C struct by value spelled `40 bytes` (number times a type = fixed array of the type, via implicit multiplication; general rule in wiki/number.md), not bytes<N>\n\nuser 2026-11-25 refinement: spelled with the multiplication dot, `40⋅byte` (⋅ already aliases *), so it is not read as two list items; singular is fine, plural only if free\n\nstanding OK small overrun 2026-11-25 (wap-interviewer): n⋅T rule core +4 vs ~3 (type_notation +2, analyze/shapes +2); prefer vector(T, n) if free\n\nstanding OK small overrun: 4 core lines (shapes.wap shaped, type_notation.wap array_item) for `n⋅T` → list<T>, wap-interviewer 2026-11-25; spelling `40⋅byte` by the user 2026-11-25; wiki 01512ea on master\n\nland: native-other-platforms 86d1681c (handed to wap-integrator); proof on x86-64: test_c on the next Linux run, then --known\n\nseen: glue.awk matches only import modules named [a-z0-9_]+, so a native executable (build.sh) cannot link a C function `from '/path/libx.dylib'` (undefined symbol w2c_0x2F...); the native wap runs it fine\n\nlanded f1ca88a4 (x86-64 struct by value, `40⋅byte`, n⋅T rule); left: Windows, Linux window; x86-64 proof by test_c on the next Linux run\n\nReleased 2026-11-25 by wap-annotation-attachment-fix: done: C structs by value on x86-64 and ARM64 (40⋅byte), f1ca88a4; left: Windows, the Linux window, and the x86-64 proof by test_c on the next Linux run (then --known); branch: none\n\nland: glue-any-module dc00c293 (handed to wap-integrator): glue.awk takes any module name (a library's path included), test_native line; GPU window surface on Wayland/X11 (lib only). Left: Windows; Linux window proof on a Linux run with a display\n\nlanded e9e413e5: C from a library's path in native builds; Wayland/X11 GPU surface (compile-checked; needs a Linux run with a display)\n\n(glue-any-module's merge on main is 665da486, rebased from e9e413e5)\n\nReleased 2026-11-25 by wap-annotation-attachment-fix: done: glue any module name, Linux Wayland/X11 window surface, 665da486; left: Windows, waiting for a machine to test on (supervisor asks the user about a GitHub Actions Windows runner); the Linux window proof on a Linux run with a display; branch: none\n\nuser OK 2026-11-25 via wap-interviewer: Windows tested by a GitHub Actions workflow on pannous/wap, test_native on windows-latest, run by hand or on push to main\n\nland: native-windows 9bc927a3 — Windows workflow (windows.yml): hello green, test_native 61/63 on windows-latest; reds: console line editing (no pty/stty), standalone needs warp_wasmtime.dll (no static wasmtime for Windows in warp's release). Linux window still unproven.\n\nFixed in [9b05049b](https://github.com/pannous/wap/commit/9b05049bdac3a0c131463934ca9d87babfb54f58): merge native-windows: wap on Windows, proven by a GitHub Actions workflow on windows-latest (by hand or on a push to main): the POSIX calls programs make by name, threads linked in statically, a Windows host shim, and an absolute path opened in the root preopen whose name begins it so each drive reads its own paths (user approved the Windows workflow)",
   "state": "CLOSED",
