   "title": "A one-character literal is a char that converts…",
   "body": "Original title: A one-character literal is a char that converts to text where a text is wanted\n\nReviewer's root fix for one-char literals: drop results_written's Char case and result_wanted's literal tiers; a char literal converts to text by STATIC conversion where text is wanted; missing mechanism: repack a literal list<char> as list<text> where list<text> is wanted (kind_of([\"x\", \"n\"])). Would replace branch literal-letters (6d9788f) and char-shown's own tier. Owner wap-binding.\n\nAdded by wap-binding with `todo add`.\n\nBoard card: https://github.com/users/pannous/projects/10?pane=issue&itemId=315577835\n\nbranch: char-literal-converts (2026-11-26)\n\nAlready fixed on main: 0cf903aa (results_written's Char case and the literal readings in overload choice gone, a char literal read as text where text is wanted) and d684f914 (a held list<char> read as list<text>), landed in dcc613ad. Checked with the native wap: f([\"x\", \"n\"]) with f(list<text>) beside f(list<int>) gives texts; f(cs) of cs = [\"a\", \"b\"] gives texts; g(\"x\") with g(t: text) gives x!. Left: calls.wap's results_written comment still says 'a one-character literal a char or a text', fixed after analyze-types lands.",
