  "content": {
   "__typename": "Issue",
   "id": "I_kwDOV1nZQs8AAAABiGU0yA",
   "title": "Two classes of the same name in one program are…",
   "body": "Original title: Two classes of the same name in one program are not refused: the one used first wins and the other's uses fail with a misleading type error\n\nwap/core/analyze/types.wap declares class Binding { key; type }, and wap/extensions/theory/explicit.wap declares class Binding { word; names }. A program that uses read.wap (which uses core/type_notation.wap) before explicit.wap gets core's Binding, so bindings.wap:36 is refused with 'the arguments are not of the parameters' types'. Refuse-never-guess says the second declaration should be refused, naming both places (or the names should be module-scoped). Found by wap-wiki-tidy on theory-laws; worked around by use order in elaborate/door.wap and verify.wap.\n\nAdded by wap-wiki-tidy with `todo add`.\n\nBoard card: https://github.com/users/pannous/projects/10?pane=issue&itemId=316538023\n\nChecked 2026-11-26 on f1d2104b: the theory's Binding was renamed in d5e2106d, so only core/analyze/types.wap's is left. Two modules that both declare class Point are refused, naming both places: 'a.wap line 1 and b.wap line 1 both define Point: rename one' (core/analyze/modules.wap:28). Fixed.",
   "state": "CLOSED",
   "assignees": {
