  "column_since": "2026-11-26T17:08:44Z",
  "content": {
   "__typename": "Issue",
   "id": "I_kwDOV1nZQs8AAAABiUlkVA",
   "title": "Closure calls spend most of the compiler's time…",
   "body": "Original title: Closure calls spend most of the compiler's time in adapters\n\nA wasmtime guest profile (wasmtime run --profile=guest) of wap1 compiling wap/compiler.wap --sources at 65616ef6 puts 264 k of 485 k samples' self time in functions named 'adapter <λ or function> (Call -> …) (Call -> …)', e.g. 'adapter types/types.wap#799 λ' 38 k, 'adapter core/emit/places.wap#177 λ' 21 k. At a6abf88d it was 203 k of 336 k, so this is an old cost, not a regression. Possibly profiler attribution; if real, a direct call where the function value's type already matches would make the compiler much faster. Found by wap-seed-refresh. Tip: strip the 'markers' table before loading the profile JSON, it is 18-37 GB of hostcall markers.\n\nAdded by wap-seed-refresh with `todo add`.\n\nBoard card: https://github.com/users/pannous/projects/10?pane=issue&itemId=317450168\n\nbranch: closure-call-performance (2026-11-26)\n\nland: closure-call-performance cd2bcd6d (notes/speed.md only). Measured with perfmap + sample: closure adapters are 0.9% and lambdas 0.5% of the self-compile's running time; the guest profiler's 54% is an artifact. Real hot spots: GC 26%, text == 14%, hashed 10%, fact_of 7%.\n\nFixed in [20b8dba9](https://github.com/pannous/wap/commit/20b8dba9777fcbf202440a9319e64a146ac49a8b): merge closure-call-performance: notes/speed.md, how the compiler's speed is measured and where the self-compile's time goes (notes only)",
