 "funcref-calls-by-table": {
  "id": "PVTI_lAHOAAfgFs4BsfrazhL9B1k",
  "title": "Calling a function value looks its function up in…",
  "updated": "2026-11-26T08:15:17Z",
  "key": "funcref-calls-by-table",
  "agent": "",
  "try": "",
  "repeat": "",
  "now_by_todo": "",
  "assignees": [],
  "column": "Done",
  "column_since": "2026-11-26T08:14:59Z",
  "content": {
   "__typename": "Issue",
   "id": "I_kwDOV1nZQs8AAAABimU2_A",
   "title": "Calling a function value looks its function up in…",
   "body": "Original title: Calling a function value looks its function up in wasmtime's hash table each time: a tenth of the self compile\n\nMeasured at main 31e3fcc9 (macOS sample of the native wap compiling wap/compiler.wap): 10% of the busy samples are wasmtime's get_interned_func_ref / intern_func_ref_for_gc_heap (SipHash). In the GC build a function value is a struct {funcref, captures} (core/emit/function_values.wap value_call_code, signature_part): every struct.new interns the funcref and every struct.get of it looks it up. The memory build already keeps the function's table slot (i32) and calls through call_indirect. Proposal: one form for both builds, the table slot and call_indirect, so the branches in value_call_code, signature_part and SIGNATURE_MEMORY's twin go (likely a net shrink of core/emit). core/emit change: needs the user's OK via warp-interviewer. Measure: instructions retired of the self compile against main, /usr/bin/time -lp.\n\nAdded by wap-primitive-lookup-tables with `todo add`.\n\nBoard card: https://github.com/users/pannous/projects/10?pane=issue&itemId=318572377\n\nprototype (single module) branch funcref-calls-by-table e7f26ba4: self compile 308.3 → 282.3 G instr (−8.4%), 0 net core lines; the full version needs one table shared across modules (types module exports it, each module grows it at start and offsets its slots, ~+6–10 core lines) and gives every signature one wasm type. Sent to wap-interviewer 2026-11-26.\n\nfull version built, branch funcref-calls-by-table dbb342af: shared table, −8.4% instructions retired, core +6, kernel +3, extensions +1, size +99; all checks green (careful path). Waits for the user's OK (wap-interviewer, morning 2026-11-26); then land: funcref-calls-by-table dbb342af after a merge with main.\n\nAfter it lands: profile the self compile again (memory profiling-wap-runs). If fact_name/fact_of/meta_unwrapped are still above 5% of self time, reopen fact-ids with option A (interviewer, 2026-11-26).\n\nuser 2026-11-26 via interviewer: yes, land it (+10 across core/emit, kernel, native; self-compile −8.4%).\n\nInterviewer correction 2026-11-26: functions keep their types. Each signature has its own wasm function type (signature_function_index), and call_indirect checks it on every call. 'one wasm type per signature' had been misread as 'all signatures share one type'.\n\nland: funcref-calls-by-table 949ca059 (user yes 2026-11-26). Careful path on merge 70bc1918 (main c4469b50): areas 245 ✓, native 66 ✓, self 95 ✓, census ✓ with size ceiling 174660 (+122 nodes). Self-compile 278.0 G vs main 308.5 G instructions retired, warm: −9.9%. Patch ~/Library/Logs/warp/handoff/funcref-calls-by-table.patch\n\nFixed in [99c616fb](https://github.com/pannous/wap/commit/99c616fb8f3d45491897f70464e60238fb63c6b0): merge funcref-calls-by-table: calls through function values go through one shared table, so the compiler builds itself 9.9% faster (278.0 G against 308.5 G instructions, warm self-compile); emit/wasm, emitter, values, function_values and kernel/opcodes +8 net (user 2026-11-26 via wap-interviewer; wap-supervisor: sound at 949ca059)\n\nlanded as 6528a968 (99c616fb in the done line was the unpushed first merge; main moved by a suite chore only)",
   "state": "CLOSED",
   "assignees": {
    "nodes": []
   },
   "parent": null,
   "subIssuesSummary": {
    "total": 0,
    "completed": 0
   },
   "type": "Issue"
  },
  "left_board": "2026-11-28"
 },
 "self-compile-allocation": {
  "id": "PVTI_lAHOAAfgFs4BsfrazhL9CSQ",
  "title": "Garbage collection takes nearly half of the self…",
  "updated": "2026-11-26T17:58:02Z",
  "key": "self-compile-allocation",
  "agent": "wap-primitive-lookup-tables",
  "try": "",
  "repeat": "",
  "now_by_todo": "2026-11-26T00:59:08Z",
  "assignees": [
   "pannous"
  ],
  "column": "Done",
  "column_since": "2026-11-26T03:37:53Z",
  "content": {
   "__typename": "Issue",
   "id": "I_kwDOV1nZQs8AAAABimVWkg",
   "title": "Garbage collection takes nearly half of the self…",
   "body": "Original title: Garbage collection takes nearly half of the self compile\n\nMeasured at main 31e3fcc9 (macOS sample of the native wap compiling wap/compiler.wap): 46% of busy samples in wasmtime's copying collector (forward/scan_field/collect), 9% memmove/memset, only 34% in wap's code. The live heap reaches ~1.09 GB; the null collector runs out of its 4 GiB within the first 4 s (declare + lower), so the compile allocates several GB. Heap size: GC_HEAP_BYTES (extensions/native/run.wap) 128 MB → 1 GB retires 7–14% fewer instructions on the self compile, peak RSS unchanged (1.09 GB) but compiling hello 151 → 221 MB; a memory-for-speed trade for the user. The lasting fix is allocating less: the top allocating callers in the profile were text + (place_key, join in lower/is_kind_word, ascii_lower via lower), contains/index_where/filter in functions_typed_from. Tools: data/profile/{merged.wasm recipe, tree.py} in /Volumes/Worktrees/derived-words-library (wasm-merge the types module + program + a _start glue, -g, --disable-compact-imports with explicit features; run with wasmtime --profile perfmap -C collector=copying -O gc-heap-initial-size=…; /usr/bin/sample (Homebrew's sample shadows it)).\n\nAdded by wap-primitive-lookup-tables with `todo add`.\n\nBoard card: https://github.com/users/pannous/projects/10?pane=issue&itemId=318572836\n\ninterviewer 2026-11-26 night: A, allocate less (not B, the 1 GB start heap). Cuts outside core land at once; core cuts that are pure shrinks fall under the standing OK, anything bigger goes to the user with its numbers\n\nbranch: self-compile-allocation (2026-11-26)\n\nland: self-compile-allocation ef7b5d26\n\nnext cut: the list constants of wap/types written with := (LEAD_BITS, BLANK_BYTES, REGIONAL_INDICATORS, GRAPHEME_EXTENDING in texts.wap; EXPONENT_BYTES in numbers.wap) are charged (wiki/assignment.md: evaluated at every use), so each read builds the list again; LEAD_BITS once per decoded character. Try = (computed once) if the types module allows globals there.\n\nlanded: 43f722a4 + 559397b8 (−11.4%). Dropped: the := list constants of wap/types: := is the language's way to write constant lists everywhere (lib/ too), fresh at each use so a mutable list is never shared; LEAD_BITS costs ~0.1%. Left on this card: is_kind_word lowers a case name at each call (core, small).\n\nland: self-compile-allocation-2 fe55f461\n\nafter the next seed refresh: has_fact (core/stage.wap) moves into wap/nodes.wap beside attributes_of, and without_attribute's check uses it (the seed's types module lacks it today).\n\nFixed in [6568b5d7](https://github.com/pannous/wap/commit/6568b5d71b57937bfe27b6205b739217c0089f6c): merge self-compile-allocation-2: the self compile retires 5.3% fewer instructions (309.2 → 292.9 G): text + gives back the other side of an empty one, encoded reads its lead bits once, without_attribute gives back a node holding no such fact (nodes.wap 0 net, wap-supervisor sound); +49 nodes\n\nclosed with the total: the self compile 353.8 → 292.9 G instructions retired on main (−17%): round 1 −11.4% (ascii_lower one text, functions_typed_from by key), facts-by-name-speed −1.4%, round 2 −5.3% (text + of an empty side, encoded's lead bits once, without_attribute of an absent fact). Later cuts fell below 1% (signature_key −0.07%). funcref-calls-by-table (−8.4% more) waits for the user.\n\ndup?: native-gc-heap (by score, 2026-11-26)\n\nuser 2026-11-26 via interviewer: OK to land (gc heap 2 GB 0 core / fact ids +3 core / no double prepare +3 core)\n\nProfile at main a2107d22 plus the interned-names steps (perfmap + sample, running self time): wasmtime's copying collector 27%, memmove 4.6%, memset 3.2%, with GC_HEAP_BYTES at 2 GB. The next lever after interned-names (notes/speed.md).",
   "state": "CLOSED",
   "assignees": {
    "nodes": [
     {
      "login": "pannous"
     }
