  "content": {
   "__typename": "Issue",
   "id": "I_kwDOV1nZQs8AAAABimjzUA",
   "title": "A loop variable that reuses an outer name changes…",
   "body": "Original title: A loop variable that reuses an outer name changes that name's type without saying so\n\nMet in wap/core/analyze/types.wap typed_among: the round holds `typed = try typed_form(program, …)` (a Block); a later line `for typed in s.functions { instance_indexed(typed, s) }` rebinds the same variable to each TypedFunction, so `typed` widens to any, and the refusal lands two lines on, far from the cause: 'types.wap line 196:24: this pattern can never fit a value of type any: statements = values + statements_in(typed)'. Question for the card: refuse at the `for` (naming the outer variable and both types), or give the loop variable its own scope (wiki: which does the variables page say?). Either way, the refusal points at the reuse.\n\nAdded by wap-primitive-lookup-tables with `todo add`.\n\nBoard card: https://github.com/users/pannous/projects/10?pane=issue&itemId=318585770\n\nbranch: loop-variable-reuses-name (2026-11-26)\n\nland: loop-variable-reuses-name c4ed64d4 (standing OK small fix, +1 net core; wap-supervisor: sound)\n\nFixed in [da92db88](https://github.com/pannous/wap/commit/da92db889151aa69f52e65289e8da04388a2c709): test: the size ceiling is the measured 172677 after loop-variable-reuses-name (+24: the refusal names where a name came to hold any, core +1)",
