  "content": {
   "__typename": "Issue",
   "id": "I_kwDOV1nZQs8AAAABioVZnQ",
   "title": "wap format drops the spaces inside a class's…",
   "body": "Original title: wap format drops the spaces inside a class's braces, so the compiler's own source is not format-clean\n\nFound 2026-11-26 by the T3b worker: main's compiler (dc97d6d9) formats wap/core/analyze/names.wap line 11 `class Definition { name: text; … }` as `class Definition {name: text; … }`, and so on for every one-line class: every core file differs from its formatted self, so the landing check 'wap format on the files you wrote' would rewrite whole files.\n\nAdded by wap-theory-reads-types with `todo add`.\n\nBoard card: https://github.com/users/pannous/projects/10?pane=issue&itemId=318695090\n\nbranch: class-spaces-wap (2026-11-26)\n\nbranch class-spaces-wap tip d0d5224c: option A built (format.wap +2, 13 test expectations in their own commits); reader+operators green, hello green. Waiting for the user's OK (interviewer, morning); do not land before.\n\nuser 2026-11-26 via interviewer: A, format writes one-line braces spaced { a: 1 } (+2 core); the ~10 test_reader/test_operators expectation edits are granted (own commit).\n\nland: class-spaces-wap 79b8c9e9 (user decision 2026-11-26, A)\n\nFixed in [4257fc11](https://github.com/pannous/wap/commit/4257fc1161f61810ed98722d46e78dc86e3708d8): merge class-spaces-wap: wap format writes one-line braces with spaces inside (class X { a: int }, if c { 1 }), (), [] and empty {} stay tight; the reader, operators and native expectations follow (user 2026-11-26 via wap-interviewer)",
