 "standard-word-cost": {
  "id": "PVTI_lAHOAAfgFs4BsfrazhMFFHE",
  "title": "Calling one word from a types file a module…",
  "updated": "2026-11-26T13:43:02Z",
  "key": "standard-word-cost",
  "agent": "",
  "try": "",
  "repeat": "",
  "now_by_todo": "",
  "assignees": [],
  "column": "Done",
  "column_since": "2026-11-26T13:43:01Z",
  "content": {
   "__typename": "Issue",
   "id": "I_kwDOV1nZQs8AAAABiucaDQ",
   "title": "Calling one word from a types file a module…",
   "body": "Original title: Calling one word from a types file a module doesn't use makes the compile of wap 12% costlier\n\nMeasured 2026-11-26, one compiler (wap1 of 6528a968) running --sources compiler.wap on source copies that differ in one line of wap/extensions/wasm/modules.wap linked_name: base 345 G instructions; (absolute_path(path) ?? path) — absolute_path is in types/host.wap, which modules.wap does not use — 388-389 G (+12%), also through a helper function; (folder_of(path) + \"/\" + file_name(path)) 345 G. real_path, the other word of host.wap, is refused instead ('add use \"types/host.wap\"'). So a free word brought in from a types file costs about 43 G, out of all proportion to one call: the bringing (core/analyze/modules.wap standard_word_bound, pipeline standard_sources) probably reads and types the file or the types module again. Probe copies: /Volumes/Worktrees/speed-profile/data/inp-{base,modules,m1,m3}.\n\nAdded by wap-primitive-lookup-tables with `todo add`.\n\nBoard card: https://github.com/users/pannous/projects/10?pane=issue&itemId=319100017\n\nCause (profiled 2026-11-26): core/pipeline.wap typed_modules prepares every program module (modules_prepared), and when standard_named finds a standard word it brings the module in and calls typed_modules again, preparing all modules anew: the second pass is the only new subtree in the profile (+7372 samples, typed_modules → modules_prepared → flat_map at pipeline.wap:233/258); +43 G native, +61 G under wasmtime for one standard word in the compile of wap. Fix (core, ~+3-5): keep the prepared statements of modules whose binding does not change; prepare again only the brought modules and those that named a standard word.\n\nPlan for the user's OK, built and measured on branch standard-word-cost cc2246b9 (not handed off): typed_modules and modules_prepared (core/pipeline.wap) take a map kept: each module's prepared statements by its file. When standard_named finds standard words, the modules naming them are removed from kept (one loop over the named words), and the pass again prepares only those and the brought modules. Soundness: a module's preparation reads only its own uses and named words and the signatures of the modules it uses; bringing a module in adds it and changes uses/named only of the modules naming its words (standard_used), never a signature, so every other module prepares to the same statements. Fixpoint: a brought module naming further standard words starts another pass the same way, each pass preparing only new and naming modules, so the work stays linear; it stays one filter (the remove loop) on the existing restart. Core: +3 net code lines (+7 −4). Measured: the compile of wap with one standard word (wap1 --sources) 405.9 G -> 370.2 G instructions retired, warm (−8.8%); the module written is the same (byte-identical up to the folder name).\n\nuser 2026-11-26 via interviewer: OK to land (gc heap 2 GB 0 core / fact ids +3 core / no double prepare +3 core)\n\nuser yes 2026-11-26 via interviewer (+3 core). land: standard-word-cost f0f5187f. modules/use_page/sound/cli/functions_library 107 ✓ (only test_sound_wanted red, a wanted file), self 95 ✓, census ✓ with ceiling 175679 (+66).\n\nFixed in [bc54a7c4](https://github.com/pannous/wap/commit/bc54a7c4f046b96986114e9fc4449ae2fc32dbb5): merge standard-word-cost: after standard words are brought in, the pass prepares again only the brought modules and those naming their words, keeping every other module's statements; the compile of wap with one standard word retires 8.8% fewer instructions, the module written the same (+3 net core in core/pipeline.wap; user 2026-11-26 via wap-interviewer)",
